ML_PAGE("l2-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l2\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 2 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Chapter 2 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 31 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with the answer, the logic and the exam trap hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>31</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>31</strong><span>Exam traps explained</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l2\" data-quiz=\"2\">Start Chapter 2 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l2\" id=\"l2p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">5 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - EXAM STRATEGY</li><li data-full=\"Your practice examination\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s2\" title=\"Your practice examination\">Your practice examination</button></li><li data-full=\"Three principles to remember\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s3\" title=\"Three principles to remember\">Three principles to remember</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Practice questions (Q1-Q31)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s5\" title=\"Practice questions (Q1-Q31)\">Practice questions (Q1-Q31)</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"Numerical formulas you should know\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s7\" title=\"Numerical formulas you should know\">Numerical formulas you should know</button></li><li data-full=\"How to diagnose your exam mistakes\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s8\" title=\"How to diagnose your exam mistakes\">How to diagnose your exam mistakes</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l2\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 2 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Exam strategy, practice questions, formula sheets and review - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>5 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 2 - MCQ Exam Preparation</h2>\n<p>Fundamentals of Investing · Securities Markets and Transactions</p>\n<p>Chapter 2 is especially suitable for difficult MCQs because many concepts sound almost identical but have fundamentally different economic consequences.</p>\n<p>For example, a broker and a dealer both facilitate trading, but only the dealer normally acts as the counterparty in the textbook's distinction. Similarly, buying a stock using margin and short-selling a stock both involve borrowing, but the obligations and potential losses are quite different.</p>\n<p>I've built this examination around your Chapter 2 slides, covering all six learning objectives: market types, IPOs, trading systems, international markets, regulation and securities transactions.</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l2p-d1\">PART A - EXAM STRATEGY</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Your practice examination</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p><strong>Questions:</strong> 30 · <strong>Suggested time:</strong> 45 min · <strong>Target score:</strong> 80%</p>\n<p>Questions progress from definitions and applications to numerical calculations, conceptual traps and investment decisions. Select the single best answer. After submitting, you can review every question, see your score and learn the reasoning behind each correct answer.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Three principles to remember</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><ol>\n<li>\n<p>Follow the economic transaction. When classifying a market or intermediary, ask who receives the money, who owns the security and who bears the risk.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Separate market direction from position structure. A stock rising 20% does not mean every investor makes 20%. Cash buyers, leveraged buyers and short sellers experience different outcomes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Look for hidden trade-offs. Underwriting transfers distribution risk; dealers provide liquidity while assuming inventory exposure; leverage magnifies both gains and losses.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For numerical questions, unless otherwise stated, ignore taxes, brokerage commissions, loan interest and other transaction costs.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l2p-d4\">PART B - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Practice questions (Q1-Q31)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>A corporation issues a new 10-year bond to raise money for factory expansion. An investor purchases the bond directly from the new offering. Which classification is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Primary money market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Secondary capital market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Primary capital market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Secondary money market</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The bond is newly issued, so the transaction is in the primary market. Its 10-year maturity makes it a capital-market security. Primary/secondary and money/capital are separate classification dimensions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Always classify the issuance status and maturity separately.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>Investor A sells 1,000 existing shares of ABC Berhad to Investor B through a stock exchange. Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> ABC Berhad receives the sales proceeds as new capital.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Investor A receives the proceeds; the transaction occurs in the secondary market.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The transaction must be classified as a primary-market rights offering.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Investor B becomes a lender rather than a shareholder.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>In a normal secondary-market resale, the seller receives the proceeds. The company does not directly raise new capital from that resale, even though secondary-market trading supports liquidity and price discovery.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Follow the money: who actually receives the transaction proceeds?</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>A government issues a new Treasury bill maturing in 90 days. An investor buys it at issuance. Which market classification is most appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Primary money market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Secondary capital market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Primary capital market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Secondary money market</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A newly issued instrument belongs to the primary market, and a 90-day maturity places the Treasury bill in the money market.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A government security is not automatically a long-term capital-market instrument.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>A student claims secondary markets are economically unimportant because companies receive no new funds when previously issued shares are resold. What is the strongest response?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The student is correct because resale trading serves no economic function.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Secondary markets create new company shares every time an investor buys.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Secondary markets guarantee the price of newly issued shares.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Secondary markets improve liquidity and price discovery, making initial investment more attractive.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The slides identify liquidity and continuous pricing as central secondary-market functions. Investors may be more willing to buy newly issued securities when they expect to have a practical way to sell later.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A market can add economic value without directly transferring fresh capital to the issuer.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>What distinguishes an initial public offering (IPO) from an ordinary secondary-market purchase of shares?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> An IPO is the first public sale of a company's stock.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> An IPO occurs only when existing investors trade among themselves.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> An IPO always sells corporate bonds rather than shares.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An IPO guarantees positive capital gains to subscribing investors.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The chapter defines an IPO as the first public sale of company stock. A secondary-market purchase involves securities that have already been issued.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Going public is not the same as routine trading after the company is public.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>A listed corporation gives its existing shareholders the opportunity to subscribe for additional newly issued shares in proportion to their existing holdings. Which primary-market method best describes this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Private placement</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Secondary-market resale</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Rights offering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Short sale</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter identifies rights offerings as a primary-market distribution alternative. They provide existing shareholders with subscription rights to purchase newly issued shares.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Issuing additional shares to existing owners is not simply a secondary-market transaction.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>A company sells a new securities issue privately to a small group of selected institutional investors rather than broadly offering it to the public. Which method best describes the transaction?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Rights offering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Private placement</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Fourth-market resale</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Ordinary secondary-market exchange trade</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter lists private placement as a primary-market issuance method. Here the new issue is offered to a selected group instead of being broadly distributed through a public offering.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Private placement concerns how newly issued securities are distributed; it is not synonymous with secondary trading.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>During an IPO waiting period, prospective investors receive a preliminary offering document containing information about the company and proposed issue. What is this document called?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Tombstone</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Final audit report</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Selling-group agreement</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Red herring</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The slides define a red herring as the preliminary prospectus available during the waiting period. The final prospectus is the broader formal disclosure document describing the issue and issuer.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse the preliminary prospectus with the roadshow, tombstone or final prospectus.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>An investment bank purchases a securities issue from the company at an agreed price and assumes the risk of reselling it to investors. What is the bank primarily doing?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Acting as a guaranteed investment-return provider</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Operating as a short seller of the issuing company's shares</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Underwriting the offering and accepting distribution risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Eliminating all risks associated with the issuing company</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Under the chapter's described underwriting arrangement, the investment bank purchases the issue and bears the financial risk of reselling it. This does not eliminate the issuer's business risk or guarantee returns to public investors.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish underwriting distribution risk from the investment risk borne by shareholders.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>A large IPO involves several investment banks sharing underwriting risk, while other brokerage firms help sell the shares to investors. Which distinction is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The underwriting syndicate shares financial risk; the selling group helps distribute securities.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The selling group guarantees all future investor returns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The underwriting syndicate exists solely to regulate the company's accounting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The selling group and underwriting syndicate have no different responsibilities.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The chapter distinguishes the underwriting syndicate, which shares underwriting risk, from the selling group, which assists in marketing and distributing the securities.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Risk sharing and distribution assistance are related functions, but not the same responsibility.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>In Transaction A, an intermediary matches a buyer with a seller. In Transaction B, an intermediary purchases securities from a seller and later resells them from its own inventory. What are the roles?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A is dealer; B is broker</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both are brokers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both are dealers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A is broker; B is dealer</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A broker primarily facilitates trades between investors. A dealer trades as principal and acts as a counterparty. The dealer may bear inventory and market-price risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The central distinction is whether the intermediary merely matches the trade or becomes a trading counterparty.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>A dealer quotes a bid of RM50.80 and an ask of RM51.20. You want to immediately sell 100 shares to the dealer. What is the gross amount received?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM5,120</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM5,080</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM5,100</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM40</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The bid is the price the dealer offers to pay when buying. Selling 100 shares at RM50.80 gives 100 x RM50.80 = RM5,080, before transaction costs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Bid and ask quotations are defined from the market maker's perspective.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>A dealer quotes RM40.00 bid and RM40.40 ask. Ignoring other costs and price changes, an investor buys 100 shares from the dealer and immediately sells them back. What is the investor's loss?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM20</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM40</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM80</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The investor buys at the ask for RM4,040 and sells at the bid for RM4,000. The RM40 difference reflects the bid-ask spread for 100 shares.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Even without a change in quoted market prices, crossing the spread creates a transaction cost.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>Two pension funds execute a large securities transaction directly with each other through an electronic communications network, bypassing a market maker. Which market structure best matches the textbook?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fourth market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Third market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Primary market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Traditional dealer market</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The fourth market involves institutional investors transacting directly with one another, often using ECNs to bypass market makers. The third market involves institutional trading through non-exchange-member market makers.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Third market uses a market maker; fourth market bypasses one.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>Under the chapter's historical description, an investor is considering a small, thinly traded company quoted on the OTC Pink Sheets. Which concern is most consistent with the slides?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The company must satisfy the strictest exchange-listing rules.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The security is guaranteed to be as liquid as a large NYSE stock.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The security cannot experience a capital loss.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Liquidity may be extremely low and the investment may be highly risky.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The chapter describes Pink Sheets securities as small, frequently illiquid and potentially highly speculative. Its regulatory descriptions are historical and should not be treated as a current universal legal rule.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Trading access does not imply liquidity, listing quality or investor protection.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>Which combination most closely describes a bull market in the chapter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Falling prices, pessimism and economic slowdown</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Rising prices, optimism and favorable market conditions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Flat prices, zero volatility and guaranteed dividends</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Rising bond coupons and falling economic activity</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The textbook associates bull markets with rising security prices, optimism and generally favorable market conditions, while bear markets involve falling prices and pessimism.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Market sentiment and broad direction are not guarantees about every security.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>Why can an internationally diversified portfolio have lower overall risk than a portfolio invested entirely in one domestic market?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> International securities always have higher returns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Foreign currencies eliminate financial risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Different markets may move imperfectly together, reducing portfolio concentration.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Foreign governments guarantee investors against losses.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter explains that international markets do not necessarily move in the same direction or at the same time. Different economic cycles can provide diversification benefits, although new risks are introduced.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Diversification benefit depends on return relationships, not merely owning foreign securities.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>A Malaysian investor buys a U.S. stock. Its USD value rises 10%, but the USD depreciates 12% against MYR during the holding period. Ignoring distributions and costs, what is the MYR return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> +22%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> -2%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> +3.2%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> -3.2%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The correct currency-adjusted return is (1.10 x 0.88) - 1 = -3.2%. A gain in the asset's foreign currency can be more than offset by depreciation of that currency against the investor's home currency.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not simply add a stock return and an exchange-rate movement when calculating the exact combined return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>A U.S. investor wants exposure to a foreign company's shares through a U.S.-traded depositary instrument. Which instrument best matches the chapter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> American Depositary Receipt (ADR)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> U.S. Treasury bill</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Corporate commercial paper</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A domestic government savings account</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The chapter identifies ADRs as dollar-denominated receipts representing shares of foreign companies held by a depositary bank, providing a route to foreign-equity exposure.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Trading or being denominated in USD does not eliminate exposure to the underlying foreign investment.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>A foreign government unexpectedly changes securities taxation and capital-transfer restrictions, impairing overseas investors' ability to recover proceeds. Which risk category is most directly illustrated?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Only ordinary stock-price volatility</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Government-policy and political/regulatory risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only broker inventory risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No investment risk because the assets remain foreign</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter identifies government-policy risk from foreign laws, regulation and political conditions. Currency risk can also affect international investing, but the scenario principally concerns legal and policy changes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Differentiate changes in exchange rates from changes in government rules.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>A company preparing a U.S. public securities issue is subject to a law emphasizing disclosure about the issuer and offering. Which textbook law is the best match?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Maloney Act of 1938</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Securities Act of 1933</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Insider Trading and Fraud Act of 1988</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter associates the Securities Act of 1933 with securities-offering disclosure. Sarbanes-Oxley emphasizes accounting and audit controls, while the other laws address different regulatory issues.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The 1933 Act focuses on disclosure; the 1934 Act established the SEC in the chapter.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>Which statement correctly distinguishes the Securities Act of 1934 from the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 in the slides?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The 1934 Act established the SEC; Sarbanes-Oxley strengthened accounting and audit requirements.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both laws were introduced specifically to govern short-selling margins.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The 1934 Act created all mutual funds; Sarbanes-Oxley established the Nasdaq market.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Sarbanes-Oxley eliminated the need for financial reporting.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>According to the chapter, the 1934 Act established the SEC as the federal regulator, while Sarbanes-Oxley tightened accounting and audit guidelines to reduce corporate fraud.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Learn each regulation by the market failure or institutional function it addresses.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>A corporate executive trades shares using material nonpublic information about an impending earnings announcement. Which statement best matches the chapter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The activity is automatically permitted because the executive owns the shares.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is a form of ordinary diversification.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The trade is legitimate as long as a broker places it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It raises insider-trading concerns because nonpublic company information is being used in securities transactions.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The slides identify insider trading as using nonpublic company information for profitable securities transactions. Ownership of the security or use of a broker does not itself justify use of privileged information.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The problem concerns information advantages, not the mechanics of execution.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>According to the regular U.S. trading session stated in the chapter, which time range applies to the major U.S. exchanges and Nasdaq?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., Eastern</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 9:30 a.m. to 4:00 p.m., Eastern</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 4:00 p.m. to 6:30 p.m., Eastern</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 9:30 a.m. to 4:00 p.m., Malaysian time</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The slides specify regular U.S. trading hours of 9:30 a.m.-4:00 p.m. Eastern. They discuss extended-hour trading separately. The exact extended sessions in the older slides should not be assumed current.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish regular trading sessions from extended-hours sessions and keep the timezone explicit.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>An investor purchases 100 shares at RM50 each and later sells them at RM60. The investor receives no dividends. Ignoring costs, which description is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A short sale producing RM1,000 profit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A margin trade with necessarily unlimited losses</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A long purchase producing RM1,000 capital gain</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A long purchase producing RM500 capital gain</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The investor buys first and later sells at a higher price, making this a long purchase. The capital gain is 100 x (RM60 - RM50) = RM1,000.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Identify the order of buying and selling before analyzing the profit.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>An investor buys RM10,000 of securities subject to a hypothetical 50% initial margin requirement. How much personal equity and borrowed money are needed?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM5,000 equity and RM5,000 borrowing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM10,000 equity and no borrowing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM2,500 equity and RM7,500 borrowing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM7,500 equity and RM2,500 borrowing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A 50% initial margin means the investor contributes half the initial purchase value. Thus equity is RM5,000 and the broker loan is RM5,000.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Initial margin is the proportion funded by the investor, not the amount borrowed.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>An investor controls RM10,000 of stock using RM5,000 equity and RM5,000 borrowed money. The stock subsequently increases 20%, while the loan balance stays constant. What is the gain on the investor's initial equity, ignoring costs?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> +20%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> +40%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> +10%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> +100%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The position gains RM2,000 in value. Relative to the investor's RM5,000 original equity, the gain is 2,000/5,000 = 40%. If the stock had fallen 20%, the equity loss would similarly be 40% before costs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Leverage magnifies percentage outcomes measured against equity rather than total securities value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>A margin investor contributed RM2,500 equity to purchase RM5,000 of shares. They later sold the shares for RM7,500, received RM100 dividends and paid RM125 margin interest. What was the return on initial invested equity, ignoring other costs?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 95%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 100%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 120%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 99%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Use the chapter's margin-return formula: [RM100 - RM125 + RM7,500 - RM5,000] / RM2,500 = RM2,475/RM2,500 = 99%. Financing costs reduce the return, even when the asset's price appreciation is substantial.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Include both investment income and borrowing interest in the numerator, and use initial investor equity as denominator.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>A margin account holds securities worth RM6,500 and has a debit balance of RM1,200. What is its current margin ratio?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 18.5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 54.2%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 81.5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 120.0%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Margin = (V - D)/V = (6,500 - 1,200)/6,500 = 5,300/6,500 ≈ 81.5%. This measures investor equity as a proportion of current securities value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not calculate borrowed funds divided by value and call that the investor's margin.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>A short seller borrows and sells 100 shares at RM50 each. Later they repurchase the shares for RM30 and return them to the lender. Ignoring all costs, what is the profit?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM2,000 profit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM2,000 loss</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM3,000 profit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM5,000 profit</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Initial sale proceeds are RM5,000. Repurchasing 100 shares at RM30 costs RM3,000. Profit = RM5,000 - RM3,000 = RM2,000. A short seller profits when the share price falls.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Short selling reverses the usual trade sequence: borrow shares, sell, repurchase, then return the shares.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>A short seller sells 100 borrowed shares at RM50 and deposits RM2,500 additional margin. The account holds RM7,500. The shares rise to RM70, and the broker requires a 30% maintenance margin. Which conclusion is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The margin is 50%, so no margin call is possible.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The investor has RM2,500 equity because initial margin never changes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The investor profits because the share price has increased.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The margin falls to about 7.1%, so a margin call is triggered under the stated requirement.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The liability to repurchase the shares is now RM7,000. Short-account equity = RM7,500 - RM7,000 = RM500. Margin = RM500/RM7,000 ≈ 7.14%, below the 30% requirement. Rising share prices hurt the short seller because the repurchase obligation becomes more expensive.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> For a short sale, the broker-held funds are offset by the changing cost of buying back the borrowed shares.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l2p-d6\">PART C - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Numerical formulas you should know</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_margin\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Margin trading</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Change the margin and the share price; watch equity, return on equity and the margin-call line.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter's most important technical questions concern margin trading and short selling.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Formula</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bid-ask spread</td>\n<td>$\\text{Ask}-\\text{Bid}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Initial equity</td>\n<td>$\\text{Purchase value}\\times\\text{Initial margin rate}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Current margin</td>\n<td>$\\frac{V-D}{V}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Return on margin equity</td>\n<td>$\\frac{\\text{Income}-\\text{Interest}+\\text{Sale proceeds}-\\text{Purchase cost}}{\\text{Initial equity}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short-sale price profit</td>\n<td>$(P_{\\text{initial}}-P_{\\text{cover}})\\times N$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short-sale margin</td>\n<td>$\\frac{\\text{Broker-held funds}-\\text{Current repurchase cost}}{\\text{Current repurchase cost}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Here $V$ represents securities market value, $D$ the outstanding margin loan, and $N$ the number of shares.</p>\n<p>The long-margin return example on slide 33 and the short-margin account on slide 41 are particularly worth practicing because they test whether you understand who bears the loss and how the remaining equity changes, not merely whether you can substitute numbers into a formula.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">How to diagnose your exam mistakes</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>If you struggle with...</th>\n<th>Revisit this reasoning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary versus secondary</td>\n<td>Who receives the funds? Are the securities newly issued?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Broker versus dealer</td>\n<td>Who is the actual trading counterparty?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bid versus ask</td>\n<td>Dealers buy at bid and sell at ask</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Third versus fourth market</td>\n<td>Is a market maker involved or bypassed?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IPO underwriting</td>\n<td>Who bears distribution and resale risk?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Margin trading</td>\n<td>Loan stays owed; equity absorbs changes in value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short selling</td>\n<td>Share repayment obligation gets more expensive when price rises</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>International investment</td>\n<td>Consider foreign-asset return together with currency and policy risk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A useful way to review wrong answers is to ask three questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>What economic mechanism is operating?</li>\n<li>Which assumption or constraint makes the chosen answer incorrect?</li>\n<li>What would have to change for that wrong answer to become correct?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>That third question is especially useful at Bloom's Evaluate and Create levels. It forces you to understand the conditions underlying a conclusion instead of memorizing the answer key.</p>\n<p>Source note: This examination follows the uploaded Chapter 2 textbook, including its historical U.S. market structure and regulatory framing. Numerical scenarios are hypothetical; the material should not be treated as a statement of current Malaysian trading or margin regulations.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l2-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, CFA-style, case competitions, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the chapter&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"2\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Chapter 2 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"2\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which statement defines the <strong>secondary market</strong> correctly?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The market where short-term securities are traded.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The market in which securities are traded after they have been issued.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The market where new issues are sold to the public.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The market for foreign securities.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Secondary = trading after issuance. A is the money market; C is the primary market; D is not a separate category in the chapter.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>What is a <strong>red herring</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The final prospectus filed after the offering.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A preliminary prospectus made available during the waiting period.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A public advertisement announcing a completed issue.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An illegal promotion of a stock.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The red herring is the preliminary prospectus. The public announcement of an issue and its underwriters is the <strong>tombstone</strong> (C).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which law established the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Securities Exchange Act of 1934</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Securities Act of 1933</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Maloney Act of 1938</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>1933 required full disclosure for new issues; 1934 created the SEC; 1938 allowed industry self-regulation (NASD); 2002 tightened accounting and audit rules after corporate fraud.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why are secondary markets economically important even though issuers receive no money from resales?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because they set the coupon rate on new bonds.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because they eliminate investment risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because issuers receive a commission on each resale.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because they provide liquidity and continuous pricing, which make buying new issues more attractive.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The slides list liquidity and continuous pricing as the secondary market's role. An exit route and a market price make investors more willing to buy in the primary market.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>In an underwritten offering, what does the investment bank mainly take on?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The risk that the issuer's profits fall in future years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The legal liability for the issuer's debts.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The risk of reselling the issue to the public at a price above what it paid.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The risk of currency movements.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Underwriting = the bank buys the issue at an agreed price and bears the risk of reselling it. It is compensated through the discount (spread) on the offering price.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why does a margin investor's <strong>percentage</strong> gain or loss exceed the stock's percentage move?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because the broker adds a bonus to margin accounts.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because the stock price is quoted on margin.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because margin accounts pay higher dividends.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because the gain or loss is measured on the investor's equity, which is smaller than the position, while the loan stays fixed.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The same ringgit gain or loss is divided by a smaller equity base. The loan does not change with the price, so equity absorbs the full move.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A dealer quotes a share at RM4.85 bid / RM4.90 ask. You sell 2,000 shares. How much do you receive, ignoring commissions?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM9,800</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM9,700</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM9,750</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM9,900</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>You sell to the dealer at the <strong>bid</strong>: 2,000 x 4.85 = RM9,700. Buying would cost the ask: 2,000 x 4.90 = RM9,800 (A).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>You buy 200 shares at RM40 using 60% initial margin. The price later rises to RM46. Ignoring interest and dividends, what is your return on invested equity?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 15.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 9.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 25.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 37.5%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Position = RM8,000; equity = 0.60 x 8,000 = RM4,800. Gain = 200 x 6 = RM1,200. Return on equity = 1,200 / 4,800 = <strong>25%</strong>. The unlevered return is 15% (A).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>You short 300 shares at RM25 and cover at RM31. Ignoring costs, what is the result?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Profit of RM1,800</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Profit of RM9,300</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Loss of RM7,500</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Loss of RM1,800</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Short profit = (sale price - buy-back price) x shares = (25 - 31) x 300 = <strong>-RM1,800</strong>. A rising price hurts the short seller.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A margin account holds shares worth RM12,000 with a loan of RM6,000. The price falls 25%. What happens to the margin?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It stays at 50% because the loan is unchanged.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It falls to 25%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It rises to 66.7%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It falls to 33.3%: equity drops to RM3,000 on a value of RM9,000.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>New V = 9,000, D = 6,000, equity = 3,000. Margin = 3,000 / 9,000 = <strong>33.3%</strong>. The stock fell 25%, but equity fell 50% (from 6,000 to 3,000).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>You buy 100 shares at RM60 with a RM3,000 loan. The maintenance margin is 25%. Below roughly what share price is a margin call triggered?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM30.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM40.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM45.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM22.50</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$V^* = D / (1 - m) = 3{,}000 / 0.75 = 4{,}000$; per share 4,000 / 100 = <strong>RM40</strong>. Check: at RM40, equity = 1,000 and margin = 1,000 / 4,000 = 25%.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Two pension funds trade a large block of shares directly with each other through an electronic network, with no market maker. Which market is this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Primary market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Fourth market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Third market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Over-the-counter dealer market</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The fourth market is institutions trading directly with each other, often via ECNs, bypassing market makers. In the third market, a non-member market maker is involved.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Evaluate: \"Short selling and buying on margin carry the same risk because both involve borrowing.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Incorrect, because shorting involves no borrowing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Incorrect: margin borrows money and profits when prices rise, its loss limited by the price reaching zero; shorting borrows shares and profits when prices fall, with a theoretically unlimited loss as prices rise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Correct, because both require a broker.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Both create obligations, but different ones (owe money vs owe shares) with different payoff shapes. The unlimited-loss asymmetry of the short sale is the key point.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A U.S. stock gains 12% in dollars while the dollar falls 10% against the ringgit. Evaluate the Malaysian investor's claim: \"I made 12%.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Wrong: the ringgit return is (1.12)(0.90) - 1 ≈ 0.8%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct; currency doesn't matter.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Wrong: the ringgit return is 22%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Wrong: the ringgit return is -10%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Home-currency return compounds the asset return and the currency change: 1.12 x 0.90 = 1.008, about <strong>+0.8%</strong>. Foreign asset return ≠ domestic investor return.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A friend says: \"I'm certain this stock will rise, so I'll use the maximum margin available.\" What is the strongest evaluation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Leverage raises the sensitivity of equity to forecast errors, adds interest cost and margin-call risk: a correct long-term view can still end in a forced sale after a short-term fall.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sensible, because confidence reduces risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sensible, because margin calls only happen in bear markets.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Wrong only because margin interest is tax-deductible.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Certainty about direction does not remove uncertainty about the path. Margin turns a temporary fall into a possible permanent loss if the investor is forced out.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>A company needs RM800 million quickly and fears weak demand for its first share issue. Which arrangement best addresses the risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sell the shares itself through its website.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Issue a red herring and skip the road show.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Use an investment bank to underwrite the issue, with a syndicate to share the risk and a selling group to widen distribution.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> List directly on the fourth market.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Underwriting transfers the resale risk to the bank; a syndicate spreads a large exposure; a selling group reaches more investors. The road show (dropped in B) helps gauge demand and set the price.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>An investor expects a stock to fall but wants to cap the loss if it instead rises sharply. Which combination fits the chapter's tools?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buy the stock on margin with a sell stop below the price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Short the stock and place a buy stop above the current price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Short the stock with no protective order.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Buy the stock with cash and a limit order.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The short position expresses the bearish view; a buy stop above the price triggers a purchase to close the short if the price rises, limiting the otherwise unlimited loss (a Chapter 3 order type).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You are designing a lesson to show why the <strong>secondary</strong> market matters to a firm that never sells shares there. Which example makes the point most directly?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A firm pays dividends.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Investors pay more for a firm's IPO shares when they know they can resell them easily at a visible market price later.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A firm issues commercial paper.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A firm's bonds have fixed coupons.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>It links secondary-market liquidity and pricing directly to the terms the firm gets in the primary market: the indirect economic value of resale markets.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Why do dealers widen their bid-ask spreads in volatile, thinly traded stocks?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Their inventory risk and the difficulty of finding the other side are greater, so they need more compensation for providing liquidity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Regulators require it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Volatile stocks pay higher dividends.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Spreads are fixed by the exchange.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The spread compensates dealers for liquidity provision and inventory risk. More risk and less trading means a wider spread, a higher transaction cost for investors.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A broker's maintenance margin is 30%. A client's long margin position is worth RM50,000 with a RM40,000 loan. How much must the client deposit (as cash that reduces the loan) to restore 30%?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM15,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM5,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM10,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Nothing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Current margin = 10,000 / 50,000 = 20%. Needed: (50,000 - D) / 50,000 = 0.30, so D = 35,000. Paying RM5,000 off the loan restores 30%.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>An investor buys 1,000 shares at RM20 with 50% initial margin, pays RM600 interest, receives RM400 dividends, and sells at RM23. What is the return on invested capital?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 15.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 30.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 28.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 34.0%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Return = (income - interest + sale value - purchase value) / initial equity = (400 - 600 + 23,000 - 20,000) / 10,000 = 2,800 / 10,000 = <strong>28%</strong>. Without interest it would be 34% (D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>In a short sale (Table 2.6 logic), the investor sold 100 shares at RM50 and deposited RM2,500. At what share price does short-account margin fall to 30%?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM55.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM75.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM65.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM57.69</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Funds held = 7,500. Margin = (7,500 - 100P) / (100P) = 0.30 → 7,500 = 130P → P ≈ <strong>RM57.69</strong>.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A popular stock is heavily shorted, then jumps on good news, and short sellers rush to buy shares to close their positions. What is the likely effect?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The price falls, because short sellers are selling.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> No effect, because shorts are closed off-exchange.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Their buying adds demand and can push the price up further: a \"short squeeze\" that magnifies short sellers' losses.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Margin calls only affect long investors.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Closing a short requires buying. Forced buying from rising losses and margin calls adds demand, an extension of the chapter's point that a rising price hurts short sellers, and can feed on itself.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Why might IPO shares often be priced so that they rise on the first trading day?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Underwriters always overprice.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> First-day returns are guaranteed by the issuer.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Regulators fix first-day returns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Leaving some \"money on the table\" can compensate investors for information asymmetry and help underwriters place a large, risky issue fully.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The chapter's Table 2.1 shows positive average first-day returns. A common explanation builds on its information-asymmetry and underwriting-risk logic: pricing slightly low attracts investors and reduces the risk of an unsold issue.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Leverage of 2x (50% margin) doubles the expected excess return on equity. What does it do to the standard deviation of equity returns, ignoring interest-rate changes?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Roughly doubles it, so the risk-return trade-off per unit of risk is not improved.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Leaves it unchanged.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Halves it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Removes it.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Scaling a position by 2 scales both its mean excess return and its volatility by about 2. Leverage magnifies; it does not create a better risk-return ratio, and borrowing costs make it slightly worse.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A start-up chooses a private placement instead of an IPO. Which trade-off is most relevant?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is faster and less costly in disclosure and marketing, but reaches fewer investors and gives them a less liquid security.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A private placement always raises more money.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It removes all regulation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It makes the shares trade on an exchange immediately.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The three primary-market methods differ in who can buy and how. A placement sells to selected investors: less public disclosure and distribution cost, but a narrower investor base and less liquidity.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Evaluate: \"Insider trading rules only protect the people on the other side of insiders' trades.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct, because markets are always efficient.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Incorrect, because insider trading is legal.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incomplete: they also protect the market itself, because if investors believe trading is rigged, participation, liquidity and the cost of capital for all firms suffer.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The chapter frames regulation as preserving confidence in the market mechanism, a benefit that reaches every issuer and investor.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Some economists argue short selling helps markets. Which argument is most consistent with the chapter's view of secondary markets?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Short sellers guarantee prices never fall.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Short selling raises companies' new capital.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Allowing pessimistic investors to trade lets negative information enter prices, improving continuous pricing (price discovery).</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Short sellers pay dividends to issuers.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Secondary markets provide continuous pricing. If only optimists can act, prices may reflect one side of the information. This extends the chapter's price-discovery idea.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>The chapter's market statistics (NYSE share of trading, trading hours, laws) date from 2014. What is the most rigorous way to use them?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Use them as the course's reference values while recognising that market structures, regulations and venues evolve; the durable lessons are the functions (liquidity, price discovery, disclosure).</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Treat them as current facts.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Ignore the chapter.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Assume Bursa Malaysia works identically.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The notes stress separating course material from current market facts. The economic functions are stable; the institutional details are not.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Design a single scenario that tests whether a student understands \"market direction + position structure = investor outcome\".</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> \"Define a short sale.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> \"What year was the SEC created?\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> \"List three exchanges.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> \"A stock rises 20%. Compute the returns of a cash buyer, a 50%-margin buyer and a short seller, and explain why they differ.\"</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The same price move produces +20%, about +40% and a loss, forcing the student to apply the mechanics of all three positions: analysis rather than recall.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
